\begin_layout Section
Theory: Model of reflectivity
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Model-of-reflectivity"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Surface reflectivity is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $R=\left|r_{0,N}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of considered optical layers and layer 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the external environment.
 The surface reflectivity is described by the the Fresnel equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Born1980,Prokhorov1990"

\end_inset

.
 We take into account the contribution of each layer to the surface reflectivity
 by using a recurrence formula given by the transmission matrix model 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Born1980,Baeuerle2000,Bulgakova2010"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{i,k}=\frac{r_{i,i+1}+r_{i+1,k}e^{2i\phi_{i+1}}}{1+r_{i,i+1}r_{i+1,k}e^{2i\phi_{i+1}}}\label{eq:Reflectivity}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\phi_{i+1}=\frac{2\pi h_{i+1}}{\lambda}\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}}\cos\theta_{i+1}\label{eq:Phase}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 is the phase shift induced by the 
\begin_inset Formula $i+1$
\end_inset

 layer, 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i+1}$
\end_inset

 is the thickness of the layer 
\begin_inset Formula $i+1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i+1}$
\end_inset

 is the angle of incidence at layer 
\begin_inset Formula $i+1$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i,i+1}$
\end_inset

 is given by Fresnel formulas 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Jackson1999"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{i,i+1}=\frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}\cos\theta_{i}-\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}-\varepsilon_{i}\sin^{2}\theta_{i}}}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}\cos\theta_{i}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}-\varepsilon_{i}\sin^{2}\theta_{i}}}\label{eq:Fresnel1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 in the case of perpendicular polarization, and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{i,i+1}=\frac{\varepsilon_{i+1}\cos\theta_{i}-\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}-\varepsilon_{i}\sin^{2}\theta_{i}}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}\cos\theta_{i}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}-\varepsilon_{i}\sin^{2}\theta_{i}}}\label{eq:Fresnel2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

in the case of a parallel polarization.
 
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{i,i+1}=\frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}}-\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}}+\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the full complex dielectric function calculated at the layer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the angle of incidence at the layer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite that this point is never mentioned in the papers claiming 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Bulgakova2010,Sokolowski-Tinten2000,Shank1983"

\end_inset

 the use of the multilayer model for reflectivity, a description of the
 refracted angle is required, especially in the regime of interaction investigat
ed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Sokolowski-Tinten2000"

\end_inset

.
 The well-known law for refraction is given by Snell-Descartes law 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{i+1}=\arcsin\left(\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}}\sin\theta_{i}\right).
\]

\end_inset

Snell-Descartes law must be valid in case of complex dielectric functions
 as stated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Stratton1941"

\end_inset

 (and experimentally proved by ###).
 In case of total reflection, a phase shift between equiphase plans and
 constant amplitude plans appears, leading to heterogeneous waves, then
 to interferences.
 Since our multilayer reflectivity model is limited to a homogeneous distributio
n of fields in the plane normal to the surface, then we must impose a physical
 limit by which is expressed by the following condition 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}}\right|\leqslant1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This work aims to correctly describe the refraction angle as a function
 of the incident angle and validate a general multilayer model, valid in
 both transparent and absorbing media.
 
\end_layout

