.
 Indeed, the term 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 with complex valued angle can lead to the case where the following condition
 is not respected 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}}\right|\leq1\label{eq:Limit}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 thus driving the 
\begin_inset Formula $\arcsin$
\end_inset

 function out from its definition set 
\begin_inset Formula $[-1;1]$
\end_inset

.
 The angle of incidence is a complex valued quantity since it is used in
 the Fresnel formulas (Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Fresnel1"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Fresnel2"

\end_inset

) and lead to a good description only if the refraction leads to lower angles.
 Thus, we apply Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Limit"

\end_inset

 which allows to get the following results.
 
