\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SokolRef"

\end_inset

 present the reflectivity as a function of laser fluence.
 The experimental results have been reported as blue points.
 The simulation results have been reported in plain curves.
 The plain curve corresponds to calculation using Fresnel formula supposing
 that the refraction medium is optically thick.
 The dashed curve corresponds to the multilayer model developed in Section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Model-of-reflectivity"

\end_inset

, and assumes that each layer can be optically thin.
 One observe on this graph that the hypothesis of optically thick medium
 leads to a mismatch between simulation and experiment.
 However, multilayer model provide a better match and well reproduces the
 global behavior of the experiment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, the threshold fluence defined by the minimum reflectivity is
 the same for theory and in the experiment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the minimum reflectivity is slightly different.
 Indeed, the experimental value is 15%, but the calculated value is 10%.
 Regarding the knowledge we have on the model analysis we did in the Section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Model-of-reflectivity"

\end_inset

, we can conclude that the gradient of density is stronger in the case of
 Sokolowski-Tinten calculations.
 Indeed, a variation of density on a very shallow layer leads to an increase
 of the minimum reflectivity at the threshold.
 The gradient of density may be underestimated in the case of the simulation.
 This can result from the smallest mesh size taken at the surface of the
 sample, and to the boundary condition.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Taking into account the melted layer index
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As demonstrated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Sokolowski-Tinten2000"

\end_inset

, the melting processes are not important in the time-reflectometry measurements
 until a certain fluence threshold of nearly 400 
\begin_inset Formula $mJ/cm^{2}$
\end_inset

, at 625 nm wavelength and 100 fs pulse duration.
 However, at higher fluences, and especially in double pulse experiments
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Hoehm2013"

\end_inset

 at several ps delays, the liquid layer formation can play a role into the
 laser absorption.
 To take this point into account, there exist different formulations.
 A first model is to take the specular reflectivity change as a function
 of temperature 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Korfiatis2007,Tsibidis2011"

\end_inset

, but has the drawback to not take into account the response of the free-carrier
s.
 Then, we propose to base the modeling of surface reflectivity change in
 melted silicon by calculating the dielectric function of the melted layer
 following the ratio of melted liquid 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 in the numerical cell, using the following formula in a first approximation
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varepsilon=\left(\varepsilon_{c-Si}+\Delta\varepsilon_{Drude}\right)\left(1-\eta\right)+\eta\varepsilon_{l-Si}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{l-Si}=-14.85+47.88i$
\end_inset

 [REF?].
 
\end_layout

